username, because a guess that is usually right\0A/// is the worst kind of wrong for a path a program is about to write\0A/// to.\0Apub let home_dir (): Option[String] =\0A    if platform() == \22windows\22 { env_var(\22USERPROFILE\22) } else { env_var(\22HOME\22) }\0A\0A/// `None` is END OF STREAM and `Some(\22\22)` is a blank line, and\0A/// keeping those apart is the whole reason these exist. The shims the\0A/// language server uses return `\22\22` for both, so a filter reading\0A/// until end of input would stop at the first blank line, which is wrong in a\0A/// way that only shows up on real data.\0A///\0A/// The line arrives WITHOUT its newline, and a final line with no\0A/// trailing newline is an ordinary line rather than something to drop.\0Apub let read_stdin_line (): Result[Option[String], String] = unsafe {\0A    let n = stdin_line_n();\0A    if n < 0 { Ok(None) }\0A    else { match Bytes.from_cstr(stdin_line_data(), n).as_string() {\0A        Ok(text) => Ok(Some(text)),\0A        Err(e) => Err(\22Os.read_stdin_line: \22.concat(e)),\0A    } }\0A}\0A\0A/// Up to `max` bytes, as `Bytes`. Stdin carries whatever was piped\0A/// into it, which is not always text. With a positive maximum, an EMPTY\0A/// result is end of stream; a SHORT one is data, because a pipe hands\0A/// over what it has rather than waiting to fill a buffer.\0A///\0A/// A nonpositive `max_len` returns empty bytes immediately, without\0A/// consuming input or checking for end of stream.\0A///\0A/// The bytes are copied into a Plum value before this returns, so the\0A/// result stays valid across later reads; the shim's own buffer is\0A/// reused and would not.\0Apub let read_stdin (max_len: Int): Result[Bytes, String] = unsafe {\0A    let n = stdin_bytes_n(max_len);\0A    if n < 0 { Err(\22Os.read_stdin: read failed\22) } else { Ok(Bytes.from_cstr(stdin_bytes_data(), n)) }\0A}\0A\0A/// Writes to standard error and flushes. NOT `println`, which is\0A/// stdout, since a diagnostic that lands 